     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Where the cord ends:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " from the medulla @ the foramen magnum to the upper lumbar vertebrae, USU b/n L1 and L2 (conus medullaris); lower roots cont down as the cauda equina."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Anterior horns:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " hold the LMNs, driven by the descending CSTs; their axons are the efferent fibers of the spinal nerves."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Crossing:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " the spinothalamic tract carries pain and temp contralaterally in the cord; most other tracts carry info ipsilaterally."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Level rule:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " cord lesions cause dysfun @ the involved segment and every segment below it, except central cord syndrome, which may spare segments below."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Effects by level (vertebral levels):"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " @ or above C5: resp paralysis, quadriplegia; C5–C6: legs, wrists and hands paralyzed, weak shoulder abduction and elbow flex, lost biceps and brachioradialis reflexes; C6–C7: legs, wrists and hands paralyzed but shoulder mvt and elbow flex USU poss; C7–C8: lost triceps reflex, trunk, legs and hands paralyzed; C8–T1: Horner syndrome, legs paralyzed; T1 to conus medullaris: legs paralyzed."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Every level:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " below the lesion, reflexes are altered (first ↓, later brisk), bowel and bladder control = lost and sensation = lost."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Cord dysfun Eti:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " paresis, sensory loss, reflex chgs and autonomic dysfun (bowel, bladder and erectile dysfun; ⍉sweating below the lesion, more above)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Two sensory pathways:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " pain and temp travel in the spinothalamic tract; position and vibration in the dorsal columns (medial lemniscus pathway) — testing both = the most useful for localization."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sensory patterns:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " stocking-glove = dist periph nerves; single dermatome or n. branch = isolated nerves or roots; patchy sensory, motor and reflex loss in a limb = brachial or pelvic plexus; loss below a dermatomal level = spinal cord; saddle area = cauda equina; crossed face-body = brain stem; hemisensory = brain; midline hemisensory = thalamus or funct."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Confirming the level:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " check that motor weakness and reflex chgs follow the same pattern as the sensory loss."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Romberg test:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " inability to stand w ft together and eyes closed indicates impaired position sense in the legs."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Vibration:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " w a finger under the pt’s dist interphalangeal joint, the examiner presses a lightly tapped 128-cycle tuning fork on top of the joint; the pt should note the end of vibration @ about the same time as the examiner."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Joint position:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " move the tip of a finger or toe up or down a few degrees w the eyes closed; if ⍉perceived, try lrger mvts, then the next prox joint."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Cortical sensation:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " stereognosis (naming an object in the palm), graphesthesia (numbers written on the palm) and 2-point discrimination on the fingertips."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
